"Another answer was building. The Romans did not invent the arch, but they used it on a huge scale, in bridges, aqueducts, and great buildings. Over about 500 years, eleven aqueducts were built to bring water to the city of Rome. One aqueduct for the city of Nîmes, in Gaul, ran about 50 kilometers and dropped only about 11 meters along the way, crossing a valley on the three-level bridge now called the Pont du Gard. Rome's water commissioner Frontinus compared the aqueducts with the “idle Pyramids.”": "37686c72b98e",
"At the same time, at the other end of Asia, the Han dynasty ruled China. In 97 CE a Han envoy named Gan Ying traveled west toward Rome and reached a great sea, but turned back. In 166 CE, a Chinese history records, men who said they came from the king of Da Qin, the Chinese name for Rome, arrived through what is now Vietnam. The western half of the Roman Empire ended in 476 CE, while the eastern half lasted much longer. Lesson 7 looks at how the Han held their own empire together.": "7eedcb393527",
"Boudica led a revolt against Rome.": "fe736e71f083",
"But the empire was built by conquest. The Romans destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE and enslaved its people. Ancient writers such as Plutarch claimed that Julius Caesar's wars in Gaul killed a million people; historians doubt the exact figures, but the losses were enormous. In Britain, Queen Boudica led a revolt after Roman officials seized her family's kingdom. In 70 CE, Roman soldiers captured Jerusalem and burned its Temple.": "4921c0437c25",
"Egypt became a Roman province.": "e925c08e3e07",
"How do you hold together lands from Britain to Egypt? One answer was roads. A recent digital atlas maps about 300,000 kilometers of roads in the Roman Empire around 150 CE, though the exact route of most of them is not known for certain. Soldiers, messengers, traders, and travelers used them. Roads let the government send orders and armies quickly, and let goods and ideas move too.": "3ea186ac529b",
"In 212 CE, citizenship was given to almost all free people.": "2b19eb8b6c18",
"In Lesson 5, Athens was a small city-state. Rome began small too, as a city in central Italy. Later Romans said it was founded in 753 BCE and became a republic in 509 BCE, but these are traditions; the early centuries are poorly recorded. By about 275 BCE Rome ruled Italy. Over the next two and a half centuries it fought its way around the Mediterranean Sea, and in 27 BCE the republic became an empire ruled by emperors.": "a29015915fe9",
"In Lesson 5, Athens was a small city-state. Rome began small too, as a city in central Italy. Later Romans said it was founded in 753 BCE and became a republic in 509 BCE, but these are traditions; the early centuries are poorly recorded. By about 275 BCE Rome ruled Italy. Over the next two and a half centuries it fought its way around the Mediterranean Sea, and in 27 BCE the republic became an empire ruled by emperors. How do you hold together lands from Britain to Egypt? One answer was roads. A recent digital atlas maps about 300,000 kilometers of roads in the Roman Empire around 150 CE, though the exact route of most of them is not known for certain. Soldiers, messengers, traders, and travelers used them. Roads let the government send orders and armies quickly, and let goods and ideas move too. Another answer was building. The Romans did not invent the arch, but they used it on a huge scale, in bridges, aqueducts, and great buildings. Over about 500 years, eleven aqueducts were built to bring water to the city of Rome. One aqueduct for the city of Nîmes, in Gaul, ran about 50 kilometers and dropped only about 11 meters along the way, crossing a valley on the three-level bridge now called the Pont du Gard. Rome's water commissioner Frontinus compared the aqueducts with the “idle Pyramids.” Rome also spread its laws and its citizenship. At first, Roman citizens were mainly the people of the city of Rome and the lands near it. Over time, citizenship was given to more and more people, and in 212 CE the emperor Caracalla gave it to almost all free people in the empire. Much later, in the 500s CE, Roman law was collected into one great work. Taiwan's civil law belongs to the continental European civil-law tradition, whose roots go back to Roman law. But the empire was built by conquest. The Romans destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE and enslaved its people. Ancient writers such as Plutarch claimed that Julius Caesar's wars in Gaul killed a million people; historians doubt the exact figures, but the losses were enormous. In Britain, Queen Boudica led a revolt after Roman officials seized her family's kingdom. In 70 CE, Roman soldiers captured Jerusalem and burned its Temple. At the same time, at the other end of Asia, the Han dynasty ruled China. In 97 CE a Han envoy named Gan Ying traveled west toward Rome and reached a great sea, but turned back. In 166 CE, a Chinese history records, men who said they came from the king of Da Qin, the Chinese name for Rome, arrived through what is now Vietnam. The western half of the Roman Empire ended in 476 CE, while the eastern half lasted much longer. Lesson 7 looks at how the Han held their own empire together.": "96eb26fbad22",
